# f1 = total. What `list[i]` gives is anything; the three values that the pattern takes are only reckoned with, so its takers speak for a list of numbers. The stub that takes an iterable apart ran other code in the row: it
# may be typed in a clean turn, so looks count on turns that begin again. In the typed copy the value is held to a list as made (asked once), its storage to numbers, or the turn begins again in the closed copy, which keeps
# the stub; the three reads go by that kind under their written places, without a join, and what they give is typed. A row with a text, a short row and whole numbers answer as they must; a row with an iterator of its own is
# no list as made and is taken apart by that iterator.
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK: isInLoop=true isLoopHead=true
# CHECK: = IsPristine {{*}}builtinClass=Array aspects=[Storage, Names]
# CHECK: = HasArrayStorage {{*}}arrayStorage=Double [admitsInt32Storage]
# CHECK: undefined|number @OptionalDouble = GetArrayElement {{*}}arrayStorage=Double level=Proven
# CHECK: undefined|number @OptionalDouble = GetArrayElement {{*}}arrayStorage=Double level=Proven
# CHECK: undefined|number @OptionalDouble = GetArrayElement {{*}}arrayStorage=Double level=Proven
# RUN(ONCE): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# ONCE-COUNT-2: = IsPristine
# ONCE-NOT: = IsPristine
# RUN(STUB): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# STUB-COUNT-1: = DestructureIterable
# STUB-NOT: = DestructureIterable
# RUN(ANSWER): run-in-shell mechanisms=all
